{
  "id": "COMPOUND:485 - PROTEIN:813",
  "verbalization": "Oxycodone is implicated in the activation of the Kappa-type opioid receptor through a multi-step pathway involving the 5-hydroxytryptamine receptor 2C. This receptor serves as a common target for multiple compounds, including Oxycodone, Ginkgolide A, and 3-Methylfentanyl, all of which are linked to the Kappa-type opioid receptor. The evidence suggests that Oxycodone may exert its effects by first targeting the 5-hydroxytryptamine receptor 2C, which in turn may modulate the Kappa-type opioid receptor through indirect mechanisms. The high scores in all three paths indicate a consistent and robust relationship, though the exact mechanism by which Oxycodone influences the Kappa-type opioid receptor remains to be fully elucidated. The presence of multiple compounds sharing this target suggests a broader pharmacological profile, potentially involving complex signaling pathways that are still under investigation.",
